\id INT
\h Introduction
\toc1 Introduction
\toc2 Introduction
\toc3 Intr.
\mt1 Introduction
\p The present edition is a complete Bible for English-speaking Orthodox Christians. Its Old Testament is Sir Lancelot Brenton’s 1844 translation of the Septuagint, with the readings of Codex Alexandrinus that Brenton gave in his notes and Appendix promoted into the main text. Its New Testament is the 1611 King James Version as F. H. A. Scrivener edited it for the 1873 Cambridge Paragraph Bible, conformed to Maurice Robinson and William Pierpont’s 2026 Byzantine text of the Greek New Testament. The two Testaments share one register of English and one set of conventions for notes, names, spelling, grammar, and punctuation, and we link the quotations of the Old Testament in the New as tabulated by David McCalman Turpie. The result is a single volume in which the Holy Scriptures of the Orthodox Church can be read together in the traditional English of her liturgical books.
\p The present edition has a fourfold unity. It is one in witness: the Septuagint is the Old Testament of the Orthodox Church and the Byzantine text her New Testament, so the two Testaments here are those the Church has handed down, not a Hebrew Old Testament concatenated with a Greek New. It is one in language: the Septuagint was the Bible of the Greek-speaking synagogue and of the Apostles, and, as Brenton put it, “it is not only a translation of the Old Testament, but it is the Old Testament translated into the language of the New.”\f + \ft Brenton, \+it Septuagint Version\+it*, vol. 1, ix.\f* It is one in style: Brenton wrote in the English of the King James Bible, and that English, in Holy Transfiguration Monastery’s words, “has been used for centuries now with a Christian import,” so that the two Testaments read in one register.\f + \ft Holy Transfiguration Monastery, \+it Pentecostarion\+it*, 18.\f* And it is one in editorial convention: we depart from Brenton and from the King James translators by consistent rules, and document each exception.
\p Its name states its purpose: the Orthodox Liturgical English Bible. Its Old Testament is the Septuagint and its New Testament the Byzantine text; its canon and order are those of the Church of Greece’s Septuagint; and its English is the traditional English of the Orthodox services as they have been translated since the eighteenth century.
\p The sections that follow explain each choice by the method Scrivener followed in his introduction to the Cambridge Paragraph Bible: to name each kind of change, to state the rule that governs it, to give the reason and the exceptions, and to accept responsibility for the result.\f + \ft Scrivener, \+it Cambridge Paragraph Bible\+it*, x, lxvii.\f*
\s1 Liturgical English
\p The English of the present edition is the English of the King James Version: \it thou\it* and \it thee\it* to one person and \it ye\it* and \it you\it* to many, \it hath\it* and \it saith\it*, \it unto\it* and \it wherefore\it*, and the cadence four centuries have fixed in the ear. We keep it for the reasons Holy Transfiguration Monastery set out in its 1990 \it Pentecostarion\it* and has followed in its liturgical books ever since. The first is that the Church has long worshipped in a language set apart from the market: “Byzantines, Slavs, Arabs, Georgians, as well as the Latin Fathers, all composed their liturgical texts in a language that differed significantly from what was spoken in the market places,” and the distance between Jacobean and modern English is far smaller than that between liturgical and demotic Greek, or Slavonic and Russian.\f + \ft Holy Transfiguration Monastery, \+it Pentecostarion\+it*, 17.\f* The second is capacity: the characteristic words and phrases of this English “were developed specifically to render, in English, Christian writings that were composed in Koiné Greek,” and so “are well suited to represent Greek theological concepts in English.”\f + \ft Holy Transfiguration Monastery, \+it Pentecostarion\+it*, 18.\f* The third is fixity of form: a text in the English of the King James Version makes “the scriptural allusions in the text instantly recognizable, and the wealth of associated meanings such allusion carries is immediately accessible,” and the same is true in reverse: a Bible in that English lets the worshipper recognize the services in Scripture.\f + \ft Holy Transfiguration Monastery, \+it Pentecostarion\+it*, 18.\f* The fourth is that a language reserved to the Church, like her architecture, vestments, music, and iconography, marks the division between the sacred and the profane, and is evidence not of God’s distance from man but of his presence.\f + \ft Holy Transfiguration Monastery, \+it Pentecostarion\+it*, 19.\f*
\p Fr. John Whiteford gives the history of this tradition. From the first English translations of the services in the eighteenth century until the 1960s, “it never seems to have even occurred to anyone that they should translate the services into anything other than the traditional style of English that we find in the King James Version”: Orloff’s translations at the end of the nineteenth century, Hapgood’s 1906 \it Service Book\it*, Nassar’s 1938 compendium, and the \it Lenten Triodion\it* of Mother Mary and Metropolitan Kallistos all use it, and “one cannot find a complete set of service books in English that are not in traditional English.”\f + \ft Whiteford, “King James English,” paragraphs beginning “From time to time” and “Even non-native English speakers.”\f* Whiteford also makes a grammatical point: traditional English “has the added advantage of being more precise, because it allows for a distinction between the second-person singular pronoun (‘thou’), and the plural (‘you’), which is present in both Hebrew and Greek, and often this distinction is very important to the meaning of a text.”\f + \ft Whiteford, “King James English,” paragraph beginning “I would argue.”\f* This distinction is visible in a single verse of the present edition: “Marvel not that I said unto thee, Ye must be born again” ({JHN 3:7}). The Lord speaks to Nicodemus alone and then to all. A translation with one word for both loses what the Greek says.
\p Hapgood is the bridge between the King James Bible and the services of the Orthodox Church. She drew the Scripture lessons of her \it Service Book\it* from the King James Version and its psalms from the Prayer Book Psalter, “with occasional exceptions, when the exigencies of the Slavonic version or adaptation to special cases or services required slight changes,” and her book, aided by Saint Tikhon and funded in part by the Holy Synod of Russia, became the model its successors followed.\f + \ft Hapgood, \+it Service Book\+it*, vi–vii; Whiteford, “King James English,” paragraph beginning “Even non-native English speakers.”\f* The present edition sets both Testaments in that English, so that readers can read at home, in context, the words they hear in church, and recognize in church the words they read at home.
\p Liturgical English is neither a dead language nor a fixed one, and we do not treat the King James Bible or Brenton as untouchable. Whiteford, who argues that we should begin with the King James Version for the text of Scripture, does not argue that “there is no need to correct the KJV or to update it when changes to English have rendered a particular text very difficult for the average person to properly understand.”\f + \ft Whiteford, “King James English,” paragraph beginning “This does not mean.”\f* Holy Transfiguration Monastery likewise has “not adopted the vocabulary of the Authorized Version uncritically,” setting aside words that have become obsolete or changed their meaning, such as \it let\it* for \it restrain\it*, and words it judges to carry heterodox connotations.\f + \ft Holy Transfiguration Monastery, \+it Pentecostarion\+it*, 18.\f* We do the same, under the rules given below: we keep the old pronouns and verb forms, remove obsolete spellings, change a few words to the Church’s usage, and bring the two translations into one grammar.
\s1 The Septuagint
\p The Old Testament of the present edition is the Septuagint because the Septuagint is the Old Testament of the Orthodox Church, which has read it without interruption since the Apostles: the Psalter and the Old Testament readings of every Orthodox service are taken from it, and the modern printed Old Testaments of the Church of Greece carry on its text. Metropolitan Petros of Christoupolis, in his preface to the Apostoliki Diakonia Septuagint, calls it “the Apostolic Bible, to which both the Lord and His disciples refer,” and “the Old Testament, the official text of our Orthodox Church.”\f + \ft Mihăilă, “Septuagint and the Masoretic Text,” 34–35.\f* Archimandrite Ephrem Lash, reviewing a Bible that printed the Psalms from the Hebrew, wrote that “the Church’s Psalter is that of the Greek Septuagint, and has been since the days of the Apostles. It is the one used in all Orthodox services, and it forms the basis of innumerable liturgical hymns and prayers which are frequently little more than a mosaic of words and phrases from it.”\f + \ft Lash, “Orthodox Study Bible,” paragraph beginning “When we turn to the text of the Psalter.”\f* A Bible translated from the Hebrew gives the reader other words than the services use.
\p Edward William Grinfield made the scholarly case for the Septuagint in English in 1850: his \it Apology for the Septuagint\it* argues from the history of the text to its authority. The version was made in the third century before Christ; it was “universally received by the Hellenists … as authoritative and canonical, being publickly used in their Synagogues, both before and after the Christian era”; Christ and the Apostles “make their principal citations in the words of the LXX”; “the whole Christian Church, during the first four centuries, received this version, as canonical, and used and read it in public worship”; and “the Eastern and Western Church unite, in maintaining this conjoint Canon of the Old Testament.”\f + \ft Grinfield, \+it Apology\+it*, ix–xi (propositions 1, 4, 7, 10, and 15).\f* Grinfield counted the quotations. Of the 37 that the Lord himself makes from the Old Testament, “thirty-three agree almost \+it verbatim\+it* with the LXX, two agree with the Hebrew, and differ from the LXX, one differs from both, and one agrees partially with both.”\f + \ft Grinfield, \+it Apology\+it*, 30–31.\f* Of the roughly 350 direct quotations in the Gospels, Acts, and Epistles, “not more than 50 materially differ from the LXX.”\f + \ft Grinfield, \+it Apology\+it*, 37.\f* The doctrinal vocabulary of the New Testament, “Repentance, Faith, Righteousness, Justification, Redemption, Sanctification,” and the titles “Lord, Christ, Saviour, Holy Spirit,” are the Septuagint’s, “and they are used precisely in the same meaning.”\f + \ft Grinfield, \+it Apology\+it*, 24.\f* Grinfield concluded that the Orthodox Church, in holding to the Septuagint, “asserts no more than what Augustine and the early Fathers maintained and believed,” and that “the Greek Church adheres to this version, because she has received it in uninterrupted succession from the Apostles.”\f + \ft Grinfield, \+it Apology\+it*, 5, 55.\f*
\p Robert Adam Boyd’s introduction to the Septuagint gives an example of why these quotations matter. When the chief priests and scribes objected to the children crying Hosanna in the temple, the Lord answered them from Psalm 8: “Yea; have ye never read, Out of the mouth of babes and sucklings thou hast perfected praise?” ({MAT 21:16}). That is the Septuagint’s psalm, which is also used in the Orthodox Church as the Communion Hymn of Lazarus Saturday; the King James Old Testament, following the Hebrew, has “ordained strength.”\f + \ft Boyd, introduction to \+it Updated Brenton\+it*, “The Bible of the Early Church.”\f* In the present edition the psalm reads as the Lord quoted it: “Out of the mouth of babes and sucklings hast thou perfected praise” ({PSA 8:3}). The clearest case is {HEB 10:5}, where “a body hast thou prepared me” is the Septuagint of Psalm 39 and the Hebrew has “mine ears hast thou opened”: the Epistle’s argument rests on words the Hebrew does not have, and the margin of the present edition marks the quotation at both ends as the Septuagint’s against the Hebrew. Paul’s “Behold, ye despisers” at {ACT 13:41} is the Septuagint of {HAB 1:5}, and the quotation links of the present edition mark hundreds of such places. Boyd concludes: “Both Jesus and the apostles clearly accepted the Septuagint as inspired Scripture, as did the Jews of their time,” and it was Jerome, against the Fathers before him, who turned the Latin Church to the Hebrew, a path that “from the time the Bible was first translated into English, translators have followed.”\f + \ft Boyd, introduction to \+it Updated Brenton\+it*, “The Bible of the Early Church.”\f*
\p Grinfield called the Septuagint “the Viaduct between the Old and New Testament” and “the sole Canonical expositor of the New.”\f + \ft Grinfield, \+it Apology\+it*, 88–89.\f* A Bible translated from the Greek of both Testaments lets the reader cross that viaduct in English: the Lord’s quotation and the psalm he quotes, the Apostle’s argument and the prophecy it rests on, stand in the same words on both sides.
\p We chose Brenton’s translation from among the English Septuagints for two reasons. It is written in the idiom of the King James Bible, and, as Boyd says, its “classical beauty … surpasses all other English translations of the Septuagint”;\f + \ft Boyd, introduction to \+it Updated Brenton\+it*, “The Septuagint in English.”\f* and it gives the readings of Codex Alexandrinus in its notes and Appendix, as the next section explains. Holy Transfiguration Monastery also consulted it for the Old Testament readings of its \it Triodion\it*.
\s1 Brenton’s Translation and Codex Alexandrinus
\p Brenton translated the Vatican text of the Septuagint, in Valpy’s printed edition, which descends from the 1587 Roman edition and so, with some supplements and corrections, from Codex Vaticanus. His title page promised “the principal various readings of the Alexandrine copy,” and he gave them in his notes and, for longer passages, in an Appendix.\f + \ft Brenton, \+it Septuagint Version\+it*, title page and vol. 1, xi.\f* He knew the question he was leaving open. “The comparative merits of the two copies,” he wrote, “have been the subject of much controversy, but the question is yet undecided. The general opinion appears to be in favour of the Vatican, while at the same time many obscure passages are rendered clear, and many omissions supplied by the Alexandrine text.”\f + \ft Brenton, \+it Septuagint Version\+it*, vol. 1, xi.\f* The present edition makes a different choice, for the reasons that follow.
\p Codex Alexandrinus has a place in the Church’s own use of the printed Septuagint. The manuscript came to England from Patriarch Cyril Lucaris, and Grabe printed it at Oxford between 1707 and 1720. When the Holy Synod of Russia first published the Old Testament in Greek at Moscow in 1821 (a 1687 Venetian printing had been a Wallachian prince’s venture), it took its text from Grabe, that is, from Codex Alexandrinus, and the first edition issued in Greece, in 1843 to 1850, was a reprint of the same Moscow Bible.\f + \ft Swete, \+it Introduction\+it*, 125–26, 184; Mihăilă, “Septuagint and the Masoretic Text,” 36; Stanojević, \+it Orthodox New Testament Textual Scholarship\+it*, 20.\f* The editions of the Zoe brotherhood from 1928 and the 1997 Apostoliki Diakonia Septuagint “opted for the Codex Vaticanus” instead, but Alexandru Mihăilă, who traces the history, notes that the Greek Church made the change “without explicitly canonizing a particular manuscript tradition,” and Metropolitan Hilarion Alfeyev states that the Orthodox Church has never canonized any text, translation, manuscript, or edition of Holy Scripture.\f + \ft Mihăilă, “Septuagint and the Masoretic Text,” 36, 39, 58–59.\f* The choice between the codices is therefore open to an Orthodox editor, and the question is which of them agrees with the Church’s own text, in the services and in the Apostles.
\p The liturgical books themselves point to Alexandrinus. Holy Transfiguration Monastery’s \it Triodion\it* states: “We have found that the Greek text of many Old Testament readings in the \it Triodion\it* that do not agree with Lancelot Brenton’s \it Septuagint\it* text in the so-called Bagster edition, do agree with the Alexandrinus edition of the \it Septuagint\it*. We have found this to be the case consistently throughout the \it Triodion\it*.”\f + \ft Holy Transfiguration Monastery, \+it Triodion\+it*, 13.\f* The Eastern Orthodox Bible project states the same preference on its website: its editorial team “has selected Alexandrinus as the primary base text for the Old Testament on the grounds that it represents the received liturgical and theological text of the Byzantine Orthodox tradition.”\f + \ft Eastern Orthodox Bible, “1 Chronicles,” “Codex Alexandrinus as the Basis of the EOB.”\f* Mihăilă’s own test of the Prophetologion in Judges 6 found it eclectic, agreeing with Alexandrinus slightly more often than with Vaticanus, 28 times to 18.\f + \ft Mihăilă, “Septuagint and the Masoretic Text,” 47–51.\f*
\p Behind the Church’s usage stands the evidence of the New Testament itself. Swete wrote in his \it Introduction\it* that “there is a considerable weight of evidence in favour of the belief that the Evangelists employed a recension of the LXX. which came nearer to the text of cod. A than to that of our oldest uncial B,” and that the citations of the Septuagint “in the N. T. and by Christian writers of the first three centuries, often support the readings of A with a remarkable unanimity,” phenomena that “point to the presence in A of an underlying text of great antiquity.” Swete adds that the Psalter of Alexandrinus was “evidently copied from a Psalter written for ecclesiastical use.”\f + \ft Swete, \+it Introduction\+it*, 395, 489–90.\f* Paul asks in Romans, “Or who hath first given to him, and it shall be recompensed unto him again?” ({ROM 11:35}), quoting the prophet Esaias. Vaticanus and the Hebrew lack the clause, and so does Brenton’s text; Alexandrinus has it, Brenton gave it in his note, and the present edition prints it in the main text: “Or who hath first given to him, and it shall be recompensed to him again?” ({ISA 40:14}), with a note that Vaticanus and the Hebrew omit it and a reference to Romans.
\p We compared every reading of Codex Alexandrinus that Brenton gave in a note or in the Appendix with Swete’s edition of the Septuagint, which prints Vaticanus with the readings of Alexandrinus and the other uncials in its apparatus.\f + \ft Swete, \+it Old Testament in Greek\+it*, vol. 1, xi–xii.\f* Where Swete confirms that Alexandrinus reads as Brenton claims, and where in the verse it stands, we promote the reading from the note or Appendix to the main text, giving the displaced reading in a note that identifies its source: \it Vat.\it* where Swete’s Vaticanus has the displaced words, \it Brenton\it* where the words are Brenton’s rendering of his printed text but Vaticanus cannot be shown to have them, adding \it Heb. agrees\it* where Brenton records that the Hebrew reads with Alexandrinus. We attribute words to a manuscript only where the evidence shows that it contains them. The same principle governs the New Testament, where a Received Text reading displaced by the Byzantine text goes into a note under the name \it TR\it* or \it KJV\it*, as the evidence allows. This is what makes the notes of both Testaments one apparatus.
\p There are about 420 such cases, of three kinds. Two thirds of them are readings within a verse. The simplest is the first in the book: where Brenton has “And God said” at the end of the third chapter of Genesis, Alexandrinus has “And the Lord God said,” Swete confirms it, and the present edition prints “And the Lord God said” ({GEN 3:22}), with the note “Brenton omits \it the Lord\it*,” since Vaticanus is missing until Genesis 46 and cannot be named.\f + \ft Swete, \+it Old Testament in Greek\+it*, vol. 1, 5.\f* Most of the readings substitute one word or phrase for another, many add words Brenton’s text lacked, and a few remove words it had. Twenty-six are passages. We print 22 from the Appendix, where Brenton gave the longer passages of Alexandrinus in full, among them the account of David’s coming to the camp and his duel with Goliath in 1 Kingdoms 17 and 18, which Vaticanus lacks, now restored to the narrative with a note that “Vat. omits verses 12–31.” One is a reordering: in 3 Kingdoms 7 Alexandrinus sets the building of Solomon’s house before the works of Chiram, as the Hebrew does, and the present edition follows it with a note at each place. Three are passages that Brenton announced but did not translate, described below. Where Alexandrinus omits a verse Brenton printed, we remove it as we would any displaced reading and keep its words in a note: the thirteen names that Vaticanus repeats at the end of {2SA 5:16}, and Brenton’s own form of the verse we print as {1KI 5:31}, which he set at the opening of chapter 6. The remarks of his Appendix that are not readings stand as notes at the verses they discuss, so that the reader need not turn to the back of the book; the Appendix itself is not printed.
\p The rest, about 125, stay in the notes: where Brenton gave the reading only in Greek and none of the translations named below renders it, where it is a remark rather than a reading, and where the evidence is unclear. At {GEN 6:2} Brenton’s note gives “Alex. angels of God” for “sons of God,” and Swete confirms that Alexandrinus reads so, but the letters stand over an erasure and Swete is unsure of the hand; the main text keeps “sons of God” and the note keeps the reference to Alexandrinus.\f + \ft Swete, \+it Old Testament in Greek\+it*, vol. 1, 9.\f* At {NUM 4:48} Brenton’s note says that Alexandrinus reads 8,450 where the text has 8,580, but Swete’s Alexandrinus reads 8,550; the reference to Alexandrinus stays a note, with Swete’s figure beside it.\f + \ft Swete, \+it Old Testament in Greek\+it*, vol. 1, 264.\f* A reading that is a plain scribal slip stays a note even where Swete is sure of it: Alexandrinus spells Noah’s son \it Chaph\it* at {GEN 5:32} and {bare GEN 6:10} and \it Cham\it*, our Ham, everywhere after, and the present edition does not make him change his name for two verses. The rule is that the present edition promotes what it can prove to be a reading, and prints the rest where Brenton printed it.
\p The same rule governs the English. We promote Brenton’s own words wherever he gave them, which is the great majority of cases. Where Brenton gave the alternative reading only in Greek, or his English for it is wrong or incomplete, we take the missing words from an existing translation of the same Greek, adapt them into King James English, and document them: R. R. Ottley’s 1909 translation of Esaias from Codex Alexandrinus, for that book; the King James Version, where the Hebrew reads with Alexandrinus and the King James words therefore render the same sense; and the 1885 Revised Version where the King James Version does not render the idiom. So where Brenton gives the reading of Alexandrinus only in Greek and notes that it is the Hebrew idiom, the present edition reads “the cause of thy people Israel as every day shall require” ({1KI 8:59}), the Revised Version’s rendering of that idiom, with “Vat. \it for ever\it*” in the note. We render grammatical irregularities in their contextual sense and explain them in notes. The present edition is not a new translation of Codex Alexandrinus: we translate nothing outside the passages Brenton’s notes and Appendix identify. Daniel is in Theodotion’s version throughout, as Brenton printed it and as the Church reads it, with Susanna, the Prayer and Song, and Bel and the Dragon in their Greek places.
\p Three passages Brenton announced but did not translate we supply from the King James Version, which renders the Hebrew that Alexandrinus follows in them. At {1SA 23:12} Brenton’s note on the verse before says only “Verse 12 is here supplied by Alex.”; we print the King James verse and name its source in a note. In Ezekiel 1 Brenton’s note gives verse 14 but leaves its last word untranslated, “at the appearance of Bezec,” transliterating, as Alexandrinus does, the Hebrew word for lightning; the Hebrew and Alexandrinus have the verse, Vaticanus omits it, and we print the King James Version’s “And the living creatures ran and returned as the appearance of a flash of lightning” ({EZK 1:14}), with a note saying whose English it is. The third is the account of Abia’s sickness and Achia’s prophecy in {1KI 14:1-20}, which Brenton’s note at {1KI 14:21} says Alexandrinus supplies. Swete prints its Greek in full; we keep the King James clauses that match the Greek, and where it differs we adapt Brenton’s English, from his Vatican account and his usage elsewhere, documenting each adaptation. We use Brenton’s names throughout. Thus verse 3 reads “And take with thee loaves for the man of God, and a cake for his children, and raisins, and a pot of honey, and go to him: he shall tell thee what shall become of the child” ({1KI 14:3}); verse 15 has the wind moving in the water and the making of an end of Israel, in place of the Hebrew’s reed and uprooting. We label displaced King James readings as the Hebrew’s where the Hebrew supports them, and otherwise as the King James Version’s. Alexandrinus omits the Vatican addition after {bare 1KI 12:24}, the lettered verses 24a–z, which tells the child’s sickness a second time; we omit it too, with a note, and print the account once, in chapter 14, under a note that says whose English it is and where we adapted it.\f + \ft Swete, \+it Old Testament in Greek\+it*, vol. 1, 708, 714–15.\f*
\p Brenton gave the readings of Alexandrinus in brief notes and in the Appendix; Swete’s apparatus records what the manuscript reads: its first hand and its correctors, its erasures, and its doubtful letters. Every one of these decisions names the volume and page of Swete that settles it, and quotes his evidence. Where Swete disagrees with Brenton, we document the disagreement and follow Swete; where Swete is silent, we keep Brenton’s note and document Swete’s silence. We bring Brenton’s Septuagint as close to Codex Alexandrinus as his own notes and Appendix allow, checking each reading against a modern critical apparatus, and tell the reader at every place what we have done.
\s1 The King James New Testament
\p The New Testament of the present edition is the King James Version because the King James Version is the chief source of liturgical English. Holy Transfiguration Monastery translates “in the English of the Authorized Version” so that its scriptural allusions are known at once; Hapgood took her Scripture lessons from it; Whiteford, surveying the English Bibles available to the Orthodox, concludes that for the text of Scripture “we should begin with the text of the King James version.”\f + \ft Holy Transfiguration Monastery, \+it Pentecostarion\+it*, 18; Hapgood, \+it Service Book\+it*, vi; Whiteford, “King James English,” paragraph beginning “This does not mean.”\f* Its Greek basis is also nearer to the Church’s text than the critical text behind most modern versions: the Received Text from which the translators worked is a Byzantine text, imperfectly printed, and the great majority of its readings are the readings of the Byzantine manuscripts. The next section explains why the Received Text is not enough, and what we did about it; this section explains which King James text we print.
\p The King James Bible has no single text. The 1611 edition was revised at Cambridge in 1629 and 1638, at Cambridge again by Paris in 1762 and at Oxford by Blayney in 1769, and Blayney’s text, with its accumulated silent changes, is the one most Bibles still print. The present edition uses Scrivener’s 1873 Cambridge Paragraph Bible, a critically edited King James text set in paragraphs, with its poetry as poetry. Scrivener collated the 1611 editions against every principal edition down to his own day, restored the readings of 1611 that later printers had displaced, adopted the corrections of 1629 and 1638 “by their simple truth,” listed the changes in an appendix, with the edition that introduced each so far as he could determine it, carried the translators’ own rules for italics and spelling through the whole volume, and set the text in paragraphs with the poetry in parallel lines after Lowth.\f + \ft Scrivener, \+it Cambridge Paragraph Bible\+it*, x, xvii, lix–lx.\f*
\p Scrivener also knew the Greek behind the text and gave weight to the Byzantine manuscripts. He reconstructed the Greek text that the King James translators followed, taking Beza’s fifth edition of 1598, one of their principal sources, and altering it wherever they had followed another edition, and published the result as \it The New Testament in the Original Greek according to the Text Followed in the Authorised Version\it* in 1881. That reconstruction, with Stephanus’s 1550 edition, defines the Received Text against which we write the present edition’s notes; these are the two editions, as Boyd observes, that people “think primarily of” when they speak of the Textus Receptus today.\f + \ft Boyd, introduction to \+it Text-Critical Greek New Testament\+it*, “The Textus Receptus.”\f* Scrivener served on the New Testament revision committee for the 1881 Revised Version, which revised the English more closely against the Greek and whose renderings the present edition borrows where the Greek they render agrees with the Byzantine text. He also held that the mass of later manuscripts could not simply be set aside: “I stand midway between the two schools,” he wrote in 1889, “inclining much more to Burgon than to Hort.”\f + \ft Robinson, “Case for Byzantine Priority,” 563n89.\f*
\p With Scrivener’s text comes the 1611 translators’ own apparatus: the Epistle Dedicatory and “The Translators to the Reader,” printed before Matthew, and the 1611 marginal notes, which give the literal Greek and the alternative renderings the translators weighed. We keep the 1611 margin because it is the translators’ own commentary on their work and the best evidence of what they meant; the section on the notes explains how we adapt it where the text has changed.
\p The King James Version closes fourteen epistles, Romans to Hebrews, with the subscriptions the translators found in their Greek editions, which say where each epistle was written and by whom it was carried: “The first epistle to the Corinthians was written from Philippi by Stephanas, and Fortunatus, and Achaicus, and Timothy.” We print them after the last verse and apart from it, because they belong to the King James Bible as English readers have known it and preserve an old tradition about the epistles; and because they are not Scripture, we leave their words as the translators printed them, respelling only their names as we do everywhere, rather than conform them to the Byzantine manuscripts. The closing notice of Acts, “The end of the Acts of the Apostles,” we omit: it is a typographical element of the 1611 typesetting, not part of the text or of the tradition the subscriptions preserve. Boyd, who gives the colophons of the Greek manuscripts in his own edition, cautions that “because these are scribal notes and not the sacred text itself, they should not be considered infallible,” and the subscription to 1 Corinthians shows why: the epistle itself says, “I will tarry at Ephesus until Pentecost” ({1CO 16:8}).\f + \ft Boyd, introduction to \+it Text-Critical Greek New Testament\+it*, “Colophons.”\f*
\s1 The Byzantine Text
\p The Orthodox Church reads the Byzantine text of the New Testament. The Received Text behind the King James Version is an imperfect Byzantine text, and the difference between the two is the reason we have revised this New Testament. Boyd tells the history briefly. The Textus Receptus, “a close relative of the Byzantine text compiled from a small number of manuscripts,” was “the dominant form of the printed Greek New Testament from the early sixteenth century to the late nineteenth century”; but “the Byzantine text is not quite the same as the Textus Receptus”: the first edition of Erasmus’s Greek New Testament “was produced from only seven manuscripts,” and “although those manuscripts were from the Byzantine family, they contained some readings that have very little support among Greek manuscripts.”\f + \ft Boyd, \+it Text-Critical English New Testament\+it*, v–vi.\f* Robinson says the same more bluntly, in the preface to his and Pierpont’s 2018 edition: in Scrivener’s or any other Received Text “many readings are printed in the main text that do not represent the dominant Byzantine consensus, nor even come close to such, but instead reflect numerous minority and weakly supported readings, as well as some readings that have no actual Greek manuscript support, but instead were rendered into Greek from Latin-based sources.”\f + \ft Robinson and Pierpont, \+it Byzantine Textform 2018\+it*, v–vi n3.\f* The Johannine Comma is the most famous case; the eunuch’s confession at Acts 8:37, the second “one shall be taken” at Luke 17:36, and the clause about Lysias at Acts 24:7 are others; in all, the two texts differ at nearly 2,000 places, most of them small.
\p Archimandrite Ephrem Lash made the Orthodox case for this revision. Reviewing a Bible that defended the New King James text on supposedly scientific grounds, he wrote that “all that they needed to say was that the Byzantine text is the text accepted by the Orthodox Church,” and that its editors “should have taken their stand on the Orthodox ground that the Church’s text is the Orthodox text, full stop.” But he added that “even if the text of the NKJV is on the whole that of the Church, it needs careful checking and revision before it can be called Orthodox,” and gave as his example {LUK 23:42}, where the King James tradition, following the Vulgate, reads “into thy kingdom,” while “the Church’s Gospel and all the liturgical texts derived from it in both Greek and Slavonic have ‘in your Kingdom.’”\f + \ft Lash, “Orthodox Study Bible,” paragraphs beginning “First of all let us look” and “Even if the text.”\f* The present edition reads “Lord, remember me when thou comest in thy kingdom” ({LUK 23:42}). We describe that checking below. The first question is which edition of the Byzantine text to follow. The Orthodox reader will expect the 1904 Patriarchal edition; we follow another, for these reasons.
\s1 Antoniades and the Patriarchal Edition
\p Basil Antoniades, for a committee of three, prepared the Patriarchal edition at Halki. The Ecumenical Patriarchate published it in 1904 and printed it again in 1912. It remains, as Jovan Stanojević writes in his study of it, “the only text authorized by Orthodox ecclesiastical authorities,” and “the most widespread text of the whole Greek New Testament in Greek-speaking Orthodox Churches,” and the Apostoliki Diakonia lectionaries reproduce it, though no council has sanctioned it or any other edition.\f + \ft Stanojević, \+it Orthodox New Testament Textual Scholarship\+it*, 3, 46, 69, 72–73.\f* Its aim was sound. The committee set out, in Antoniades’s words, to “restore the earliest text of the Ecclesiastical tradition, and in particular of the Church of Constantinople,” not from any printed edition or from the great uncials of the critical editions, but from the lectionaries and the Byzantine minuscules, the stones “rejected by the builders”; it undertook to make “no transpositions, substitutions, additions, or deletions without the support of the manuscripts, except in rare incidents but with sufficient testimony elsewhere”; and it printed in smaller type what it could not justify.\f + \ft Antoniades, preface, §§1,1–4 and 10,4–7, translated in Stanojević, \+it Orthodox New Testament Textual Scholarship\+it*, 179–80, 184.\f* Stanojević’s study allows us to assess how closely the edition fulfilled that aim.
\p First, its basis in the lectionaries is less secure than has often been assumed. For the Praxapostolos, Antoniades himself disclosed that he used continuous-text minuscules “because of the small number of the full Apostolos lectionaries and also because their text is similar to the text” of the lectionaries; 18 of his 28 usable witnesses for Acts and the Epistles are ordinary continuous-text manuscripts.\f + \ft Antoniades, preface, §5,4, translated in Stanojević, \+it Orthodox New Testament Textual Scholarship\+it*, 182; Stanojević, \+it Orthodox New Testament Textual Scholarship\+it*, 12, 93, 174.\f* The Chicago lectionary studies of the 1930s had already judged it, in Stanojević’s summary of Rife, “a poor lectionary text achieved by arbitrary methods,” and the modern collations of Jordan and Gibson find it “not particularly close to the text of lectionaries as a group or to the text of any particular lectionary.”\f + \ft Stanojević, \+it Orthodox New Testament Textual Scholarship\+it*, 61–63, 73.\f*
\p Second, it is not independent of the Received Text. Collating Antoniades’s edition and the 28 manuscripts Antoniades used at the 98 test passages of the Catholic Epistles, Stanojević found that at {JAS 5:11} Antoniades printed the Received Text’s reading against 24 of his own manuscripts; that those manuscripts “do not represent a single textually coherent group”; and that, among the witnesses collated at all 98 passages, none stands closer to his text than Stephanus’s 1550 edition, which with one lectionary agrees with it at 88 of the 98, the Bible Society’s 1830 Received Text one reading behind.\f + \ft Stanojević, \+it Orthodox New Testament Textual Scholarship\+it*, 141–43.\f*
\p Third, Antoniades printed Acts 8:37 and the Johannine Comma in small type, acknowledging their weak manuscript support. Acts 8:37 rests on a single manuscript, Lavra B 64, whose wording is not even the wording Antoniades printed, so that “it seems that Antoniades adopted the verse from a printed edition.”\f + \ft Stanojević, \+it Orthodox New Testament Textual Scholarship\+it*, 90, 184.\f* The Johannine Comma, he wrote, “did not seem appropriate to include,” being “entirely unattested in the ecclesiastical texts, in the fathers and teachers of the Eastern Church, in the ancient versions, in the oldest copies of the Slavonic and even the Latin version, and in any known Greek manuscript written independently of the Vulgate in which this addition was gradually introduced. It is retained upon the opinion of the Holy Synod.”\f + \ft Antoniades, preface, §11,1–3, translated in Stanojević, \+it Orthodox New Testament Textual Scholarship\+it*, 184.\f* Stanojević’s verdict is that the edition is “eclectic,” that “it was not produced independently of the Textus Receptus,” that “Antoniades prepared his edition without any systematic principles,” and that it “cannot be justified as a distinctively Orthodox edition, which suggests that the Orthodox Church needs another edition.”\f + \ft Stanojević, \+it Orthodox New Testament Textual Scholarship\+it*, 142–43, 176.\f* Christos Karakolis, a member of the Nestle–Aland committee, calls it “a hybrid” that represents neither a particular manuscript nor the Byzantine tradition as a whole.\f + \ft Stanojević, \+it Orthodox New Testament Textual Scholarship\+it*, 51.\f*
\p Antoniades worked under difficult conditions and closed his preface with self-criticism, saying that his edition “will suffer its own peculiar fate.”\f + \ft Antoniades, preface, §16,1, translated in Stanojević, \+it Orthodox New Testament Textual Scholarship\+it*, 185; Stanojević, \+it Orthodox New Testament Textual Scholarship\+it*, 72.\f* Stanojević proposes to build an Orthodox edition on the initial text of the \it Editio Critica Maior\it*, admitting Byzantine readings by a stated rule,\f + \ft Stanojević, \+it Orthodox New Testament Textual Scholarship\+it*, 6, 171, 176–77.\f* but this departs from what Stanojević reports as the prevailing belief among Orthodox scholars, that “the Orthodox edition of the Greek New Testament should be an edition of the Byzantine majority text.”\f + \ft Stanojević, \+it Orthodox New Testament Textual Scholarship\+it*, 54, 56.\f* We hold to that belief, for the reasons the next section gives. Robinson and Pierpont’s edition pursues Antoniades’s aim of recovering the Byzantine text from its manuscripts.
\s1 Robinson and Pierpont’s Byzantine Text
\p Maurice Robinson and William Pierpont first published \it The New Testament in the Original Greek\it* in 1991, with revisions in 2005 and 2018; its text is in the public domain, and the 2026 Jubilee Edition published by Clearview Press was prepared with Robinson’s active involvement.\f + \ft Robinson and Pierpont, \+it Byzantine Textform 2026\+it*, iv.\f* It seeks to represent the consensus of the Byzantine continuous-text manuscripts, established from collations, without the Erasmian readings and without conjecture. Its main text presents the Greek New Testament “according to its most dominant transmissional form,” and “where the Byzantine Textform itself is substantially divided, the main text reading is established on the basis of external and internal evaluation, with relatively equal alternate readings” in an apparatus at the foot of the page.\f + \ft Robinson and Pierpont, \+it Byzantine Textform 2026\+it*, vii.\f* The 2026 revision changes six readings of the 2018 main text and revises the marginal readings, while continuing the comparison with the Nestle–Aland and \it Editio Critica Maior\it* texts.\f + \ft Robinson and Pierpont, \+it Byzantine Textform 2026\+it*, iv, xii.\f*
\p The theory behind their edition is Robinson’s “Byzantine priority,” set out in the essay printed with the 2005 edition. Robinson argues from the history of how scribes copied and transmitted the text. The question any theory of the text must answer is “the origin, rise and virtual dominance of the Byzantine Textform within the history of transmission.”\f + \ft Robinson, “Case for Byzantine Priority,” 534.\f* Westcott and Hort proposed a fourth-century recension; the modern “process” view proposes gradual assimilation. Robinson argues that the former lacks historical evidence and the latter cannot explain so wide a convergence.\f + \ft Robinson, “Case for Byzantine Priority,” 534, 578–80.\f* Robinson’s answer is that the Byzantine text predominates because it is the text that was copied, “preserved by the unforced action of copyists through the centuries who merely copied faithfully the text which lay before them,” as the vulgate texts of Homer and Hippocrates were preserved; and the form of text found in the great majority of the manuscripts “may in fact more closely reflect the original form of the NT text than any single MS, small group of MSS, or texttype.”\f + \ft Robinson, “Case for Byzantine Priority,” 534, 543–44.\f* The method is not counting. The Byzantine-priority principles “reflect a ‘reasoned transmissionalism’ which evaluates internal and external evidence in the light of transmissional probabilities,” with genealogical reduction of related manuscripts wherever it can be established, with the harder reading and the reading that explains the others, and with the Byzantine text itself “not postulated a priori to be the original form of the text,” but reached “as a logical deduction from internal and external considerations viewed from within a transmissional-historical framework.”\f + \ft Robinson, “Case for Byzantine Priority,” 544, 556–57, 564.\f*
\p Boyd repeats Robinson’s objection to eclectic reconstruction. The critical editions decide each variant on its own, and the text that results, “within repeated short sequences, rapidly degenerates into one possessing no support among manuscript, versional, or patristic witnesses”; over a few verses, and sometimes within one, the Nestle–Aland text “simply [has] no proven existence within transmissional history.”\f + \ft Robinson, “Case for Byzantine Priority,” 534, 537; Boyd, \+it Text-Critical English New Testament\+it*, v.\f* The Byzantine text, by contrast, is “the form of text which is known to have predominated among the Greek-speaking world from at least the fourth century until the invention of printing,” a form of text attested in Chrysostom and long used in the Orthodox Church.\f + \ft Robinson, “Case for Byzantine Priority,” 533.\f* Boyd measures that predominance among the manuscripts that survive: where they differ, the Byzantine reading is supported by a median of 96% of them in the Gospels and 90% in Acts and the Epistles, leaving aside the \it Pericope Adulterae\it* and Revelation, where the Byzantine manuscripts themselves divide. He concludes that the Byzantine textform “is ancient, highly uniform, and well attested by a variety of independent streams of transmission,” and therefore “has a strong claim toward being the original text of the New Testament.”\f + \ft Boyd, introduction to \+it Text-Critical Greek New Testament\+it*, “The Byzantine Text,” with its notes.\f* What Karavidopoulos says for the Orthodox, that the critical editions “comprise an artificial text” never read in the liturgical life of the Church while the Byzantine text “has been read for many centuries (and is still being read) during the liturgical assemblies of the Orthodox Church,” Robinson has turned into a method.\f + \ft Stanojević, \+it Orthodox New Testament Textual Scholarship\+it*, 48–49.\f* We follow Robinson’s preference for a text with an attested history of transmission and use.
\p Robinson and Pierpont’s is one of several modern editions of the Byzantine text. Hodges and Farstad’s 1982 Majority Text and Pickering’s edition of Family 35, a late and uniform family whose readings at times represent fewer than a fifth of the manuscripts, are the others, with Antoniades’s, discussed above.\f + \ft Boyd, \+it Text-Critical English New Testament\+it*, xiii–xiv; Boyd, introduction to \+it Text-Critical Greek New Testament\+it*, “Modern Editions of the Byzantine Text”; Robinson, “Case for Byzantine Priority,” 557.\f* Robinson and Pierpont have revised their edition as new collations appeared, Robinson’s own of the \it Pericope Adulterae\it* among them, and have defended their method in print; Pierpont listed the changes the King James Version needs to follow the Majority manuscripts, and Boyd has twice carried their text into English. We follow the 2026 revision as the editors’ latest judgment of the same evidence.
\s1 The Pericope Adulterae and the Johannine Comma
\p Two passages call for separate comment. The \it Pericope Adulterae\it*, John 7:53–8:11, is in the Byzantine text and in the present edition. Here, as in Revelation, the Byzantine manuscripts divide into distinct forms; Robinson and Pierpont print the form von Soden called μ5 in their text and the μ6 form in a separate section.\f + \ft Robinson and Pierpont, \+it Byzantine Textform 2026\+it*, ix; Robinson, “Case for Byzantine Priority,” 566n97; Boyd, \+it Text-Critical English New Testament\+it*, xiv–xv.\f* Antoniades too printed it in full type, because, though absent from the daily readings, it stands in the Menaion for Saint Pelagia’s day, 8 October.\f + \ft Antoniades, preface, §10,8, translated in Stanojević, \+it Orthodox New Testament Textual Scholarship\+it*, 184.\f* The King James text has it; the Received Text differs from the Byzantine text within it at a handful of words, and the present edition follows the Byzantine text there as everywhere, with the Received Text’s words in the notes: at {bare JHN 8:9} “beginning at the eldest” where the Received Text adds “even unto the last,” at {bare JHN 8:10} “Where are those thine accusers?” where it adds “Woman,” at {bare JHN 8:11} “Neither do I judge thee” where it has “condemn.”
\p The Johannine Comma, {1JN 5:7-8}, is not in the Byzantine text and is not in the present edition. The words “in heaven, the Father, the Word, and the Holy Spirit: and these three are one. And there are three that bear witness in earth” occur in no Greek manuscript before the fourteenth century, and in those few only from the Latin. Scrivener himself, in the Cambridge Paragraph Bible, set them in italics, “now for the first time,” as a mark of “probable spuriousness,” judging that the passage “is probably no longer regarded as genuine” by anyone capable of an independent judgment of the evidence. Antoniades judged the same and was overruled.\f + \ft Scrivener, \+it Cambridge Paragraph Bible\+it*, xxxvi; Stanojević, \+it Orthodox New Testament Textual Scholarship\+it*, 167–70.\f* The present edition prints the verses as the Byzantine manuscripts have them, “For there are three that bear record, the Spirit, and the water, and the blood: and these three agree in one” ({1JN 5:7-8}), and the displaced words stand in two notes at the verses they left, marked \it TR\it*, so that the reader who knows the King James text can see exactly what we have removed and why.
\s1 Conforming the King James Text to the Byzantine Text
\p Our first instrument was Pierpont’s \it Some Improvements to the King James Version from the Majority Greek Manuscripts\it*. Pierpont, who later compiled the Byzantine text with Robinson, drew up in 1975, and revised until 1990, a list of every place where the King James Version follows the Received Text against the Majority manuscripts in a way that English can show, with the King James words, the change, and a weight by manuscript support: “only those places where the change is translatable, or potentially translatable,” and not “the many places where the King James Version may be improved in its rendering of the Greek text (but the Text itself is not involved).”\f + \ft Pierpont, \+it Some Improvements\+it*, 5–6.\f* The list has about 955 entries, 347 of them in Revelation, and it is, in effect, Pierpont’s own instructions for revising the King James Version. But Pierpont compiled it against the Majority manuscripts as the collations of his day reported them, not against the 2026 Robinson–Pierpont text, so we checked his proposals against the Greek rather than apply them unchanged.
\p We began from the Greek, comparing Scrivener’s reconstruction of the Received Text with the 2026 Robinson–Pierpont text, word by word, and documenting every difference between them as a unit: nearly 2,000 in all, including the nine places where a verse is absent or stands elsewhere. We then judged each unit alongside Pierpont’s instruction where he had one and the reports of the other witnesses that mark translatable differences, mainly Boyd’s \it Text-Critical English New Testament\it* and Thomason’s \it Far Above All\it*. Three quarters of Pierpont’s entries fit a unit of this comparison; some 240 name a place where the Received Text and the 2026 Robinson–Pierpont text do not differ, because the entry concerns the rendering rather than the Greek, or because the Majority data Pierpont worked from gave a reading the Byzantine text does not; we set those entries aside. Of the rest, we adopted more than 500 as they stand, could not apply some 35 as worded, and replaced the remainder with our own rendering of the same construction, documenting the reason at each place. We publish on the present edition’s website every verse where the Greek texts differ, with the Greek of both and, where the English changes, the words of both Bibles.
\p The places where the Greek texts differ fall into three categories. First, the differences that English cannot show, or that the King James English already carries: a spelling of a name, Δαβίδ for Δαυίδ; a movable letter; a change of word order with no change of sense, as at {LUK 1:10}; an article with a proper name; a tense that the King James future already renders. There are about 1,050 of these, more than half of the differences, and at them the King James words stand unchanged. Second, the translatable differences, where the Byzantine text says something the King James words do not: about 890 units, revised by about 810 changes of words and 40 that only mark or unmark a word as supplied, since a single change sometimes carries two adjacent units. Third, the structural differences: the four verses omitted, the doxology moved, the two verses of Matthew 23 exchanged, nine verses in all, treated below. Outside this count stand about 80 corrections of the English where the Greek is the same in both texts, treated in their own section below.
\p The standard for a translatable difference is the King James Version’s own literalness: a difference is translatable when ordinary King James English shows it, in meaning, reference, number, tense, mood, voice, emphasis, or grammatical relation, even if the sense of the passage is unchanged. We document the kind of difference with each of the 340 or so changes we decided individually, the rest following Pierpont’s instructions and the witnesses’ reports; most are a different word, a pronoun, a particle, or a word the translators had supplied in italics that the Byzantine text has in Greek, where the only change is that the word is no longer marked as supplied, and there is nothing for a note to say. An article, a particle, an inflection, or an order of words does not by itself earn its own English: a revised passage is no more literal than its neighbours, and we render a difficult Greek reading as difficult English rather than smooth it.
\p We choose the wording of a change as follows. The first source is the King James Version itself: where the translators rendered the same Greek, or a like construction, elsewhere or in the verse itself, we use their rendering, so that at {MAT 8:8} the centurion’s λόγῳ, the Byzantine dative, is “in a word,” as they rendered it at {LUK 7:7}. The second is Pierpont, whose list proposes King James wording for most of its changes. The third is the 1881 Revised Version, which is in the King James idiom; we take its rendering only where Westcott and Hort’s text, the nearest printed witness to the Revisers’ Greek, agrees with the Byzantine reading at that place. The fourth is Boyd’s 2021 \it American Standard Version Byzantine Text\it*, which conforms the 1901 American Standard Version to the 2018 Robinson–Pierpont text. We chose that edition because it is the one Boyd made against the Robinson–Pierpont text, before he based his revision on his own Greek edition in 2025, and because the American Standard Version is the Revised Version’s American form and so stands next to the King James Version in diction. We also adopted Boyd’s method, “every effort has been made to stay within the style and vocabulary of the ASV,” turning first to the ASV’s own footnotes and then, most often, to the King James Version for wording.\f + \ft Boyd, introduction to \+it American Standard Version Byzantine Text\+it*, paragraph beginning “In making modifications.”\f* We consulted Boyd’s \it Text-Critical English New Testament\it* and Graham Thomason’s \it Far Above All\it* after these, and used our own wording only where none of them served.
\p Counted by source, about 560 of the changes of words follow Pierpont; 120 take Boyd’s wording; 50 the Revised Version’s; 30 a King James parallel; about ten the \it Text-Critical English New Testament\it* or \it Far Above All\it*; 65 are ours; and one keeps the King James words themselves where a construction changed around them. Some thirty changes draw on more than one source, one of them on three. The changes of marking alone, where a word the translators supplied is now in the Greek, rest on the King James text itself. Two thirds of the revision is Pierpont’s, nearly all of it is either Pierpont’s or wording already current in the King James tradition, and our own words account for one change in twelve.
\p We judged every change, whatever its source, as a sentence. The last chapter of Revelation shows what that means. The King James Version reads at {REV 22:19}, “God shall take away his part out of the book of life, and out of the holy city, and from the things which are written in this book.” The Byzantine text differs from the Received Text at six places in the verse; four bear on the English. For “book of life” it has “tree of life,” and Pierpont, with every witness, directs the change. For the future “shall take away” it has the optative ἀφέλοι, a prayer rather than a prediction, which Pierpont does not mark but Boyd’s \it Text-Critical English New Testament\it* renders “may God take away”; we took that wording. The Byzantine text lacks the conjunction “and” before “the things”; Pierpont directs its omission but keeps the supplied “from,” and we removed both, since “from” was supplied for the conjunction that is gone. And the condition “if any man shall take away” is an aorist subjunctive where the Received Text has a present, which Pierpont proposed to render “should take away”; we did not adopt that proposal, because the King James Version renders the aorist conditional with “shall” at {MAT 18:15} and in the paired condition of the verse before, and the Revised Version and Boyd keep “shall” here. The verse therefore reads, “and if any man shall take away from the words of the book of this prophecy, may God take away his part out of the tree of life, and out of the holy city, the things which are written in this book” ({REV 22:19}), with three notes giving the Received Text’s words, and the reader can reconstruct the King James verse from them exactly.
\s1 Verses Omitted and Moved
\p The Byzantine manuscripts lack four whole verses of the Received Text, and we omit them as the Robinson–Pierpont text does: Luke 17:36, Acts 8:37, Acts 15:34, and Acts 24:7, with the clauses of {ACT 24:6} and {bare ACT 24:8} that belong to the same addition.\f + \ft Robinson and Pierpont, \+it Byzantine Textform 2026\+it*, x–xi.\f* We print no number for them, so that the verses after them keep their numbers, and quote each verse in full in a note on the verse before it, under \it TR\it* or \it KJV\it* as the evidence allows, a distinction the section on the notes draws. At {LUK 17:35} the note stands beside the translators’ own 1611 marginal note, “This 36th verse is wanting in most of the Greek copies,” which we keep because it shows that the translators themselves knew the verse was wanting. The doxology of Romans, which the Received Text prints as {kjv bare ROM 14:24-26}, stands in the Byzantine manuscripts at the end of chapter 14, and we print it as {bare ROM 14:24-26}, with a note at each place saying where the other text has it; the epistle ends at {bare ROM 16:24}. The two woes of {MAT 23:13} and 14 stand in the manuscripts’ order, which is the reverse of the Received Text’s, and keep the numbers of their places, with a note at each. The Johannine Comma is not a fifth omitted verse: both verses of {1JN 5:7-8} remain, with their Byzantine contents, and the displaced words are in the notes.
\s1 Corrections of the English Where the Greek Is Shared
\p In about 80 places the King James English says what the Greek shared by both texts does not: a case or a preposition misread, a tense, a number, a word, a construction, or a Vulgate reading followed against the Greek. Each correction follows the Revised Version, Boyd’s revision, or Pierpont where they render the shared Greek otherwise than the King James English, and the rendering it adopts is checked against the source it names; we prefer the translators’ own margin wherever it offered the better rendering, and where the renderings differ in style alone, the King James words stand. Each correction carries a note that begins “or,” and gives the former King James words, naming no witness, since none differs.
\p Most of the corrections concern the form of a word: its case, its tense, or its number. The chief of them is “Remember me when thou comest in thy kingdom” ({LUK 23:42}), discussed above, where the Greek is ἐν τῇ βασιλείᾳ, the services have it, and the Vulgate’s \it in regnum\it* had led the translators to “into.” The same misreading of a case or a preposition underlies “for him” in place of “in him” at {1CO 8:6}, which the translators’ margin had already offered, “in Christ” for “for Christ’s sake” at {EPH 4:32}, and “at the great river Euphrates” for “in” at {REV 9:14}. A tense is corrected at {2TH 2:2}, where “the day of Christ is now present” renders the perfect ἐνέστηκεν, which the translators had made “is at hand,” the opposite of the claim Paul is refuting; at {2TI 4:6}, where “I am now offered” renders the present σπένδομαι, not “am now ready to be offered”; at {MAT 25:8}, where “our lamps are going out” restores the 1611 margin and the present tense in place of the 1611 text’s “gone out”; and at {1JN 2:8}, where “passeth away” replaces “is past.” A number is corrected at {JHN 8:21}, “shall die in your sin,” singular as the Greek is; at {LUK 2:10}, “all the people,” where λαός is the people of Israel; and at {1CO 10:5}, “most of them” for “many of them.”
\p The rest concern a word or a construction. “With the fist” replaces “oft” at {MRK 7:3}, as the 1611 margin had it and Pierpont urged, and “beds” replaces “tables” at {MRK 7:4}, again from the margin; “one flock” replaces “one fold” at {JHN 10:16}, where the Greek distinguishes αὐλή from ποίμνη; “autumn trees” stands at Jude 12, “drift away” for “let them slip” at {HEB 2:1}, and “the son of James” for “the brother of James” at {LUK 6:16} and {ACT 1:13}, where the translators had supplied the kinship and chosen the wrong one. A voice or a construction is corrected in “leadeth us in triumph” at {2CO 2:14}; in “they which trouble you would even cut themselves off” ({GAL 5:12}); in “godliness is gain” for “gain is godliness” at {1TI 6:5}, where the subject and predicate had been exchanged; and in “cast off the anchors, they left them in the sea” at {ACT 27:40}, with the foresail for the mainsail. In several of them the correction is the translators’ own marginal alternative moved into the text. The Revised Version supports most of them and Boyd’s revision more than half; both support nearly half; one is Pierpont’s.
\s1 The Notes on the Received Text
\p Every change of words for a difference of Greek carries a note in the margin giving the former English, and we choose its label according to the evidence. The note says \it TR\it* only where an apparatus explicitly records the reading as that of the Received Text, defined here as the agreement of Scrivener’s reconstruction with Stephanus’s 1550 edition; where no apparatus records the place, where the two differ, or where the apparatus records Scrivener alone, the note says \it KJV\it*, since the evidence then establishes only what the King James translators had before them, not what the Received Text read. The notes take four forms: “fruit: TR \it fruits\it*” replaces the words it is about; “said: TR adds \it by them of old time\it*” puts words after them; “verily: TR omits” takes them out; and “but who: TR omits \it but\it*” takes out part of them. We quote an omitted verse whole in a note on the verse before it, and note a moved passage at both its places. The corrections of shared Greek give the former words after “or,” with no witness. The margin thus accounts for the textual changes and corrections of the English; systematic changes of spelling and grammar are described below.
\s1 The Books, Their Order, and Their Names
\p For the canon and order of the Old Testament, we follow the Apostoliki Diakonia Septuagint of the Church of Greece, with the two departures described below: the Pentateuch; Jesus the Son of Navi, Judges, Ruth; the four books of Kingdoms and the two of Chronicles; 1 and 2 Esdras and Nehemias; Tobit, Judith, Esther; the Maccabees; Psalms, Job, Proverbs, Ecclesiastes, the Song of Songs, Wisdom, and Sirach; the Twelve Prophets, Hosea to Malachias; and Esaias, Jeremias, Baruch, Lamentations, the Epistle of Jeremias, Ezekiel, and Daniel.\f + \ft Apostoliki Diakonia, “Παλαιά Διαθήκη,” table of books.\f* The books that English Bibles since 1611 have gathered into an Apocrypha at the end of the Old Testament, or dropped, stand here in their Greek places, because the Septuagint is one book and the Orthodox Church receives them, 4 Maccabees apart, as Scripture: Tobit and Judith among the histories, Wisdom and Sirach among the books of Solomon, Baruch and the Epistle beside Jeremias, the additions to Esther and to Daniel within their books, Psalm 151 at the end of the Psalter. Scrivener, who laboured over the Apocrypha of the Cambridge Paragraph Bible when few editors would, wrote that so long as the Apocrypha “shall comprise a part of the Holy Bible in its largest form, or lessons shall be selected from it for the course of Divine service, it deserves far more regard than has been paid to it in recent times.”\f + \ft Scrivener, \+it Cambridge Paragraph Bible\+it*, lxvi.\f*
\p Two books stand elsewhere than in the Apostoliki Diakonia edition. The Prayer of Manasses, which that edition does not print among the books, stands here as a book of its own after 2 Chronicles, where the Slavonic Bible and the Russian Synodal Bible print it, at the end of chapter 36, under the heading the present edition also uses, “The Prayer of Manasses King of Judah, When He Was Holden Captive in Babylon”; {2CH 33:18-19} records that the prayer was written, and the Church reads it at Great Compline.\f + \ft \+it Bibliia: Sinodal’nyi perevod\+it*, 2 Chronicles 36:24.\f* Fourth Maccabees, which the Apostoliki Diakonia edition prints as an appendix after Daniel, with a note that the Church has not numbered it among the canonical books but publishes it for the importance of its contents and its presence in the ancient manuscripts, stands here with the other three books of Maccabees, as it stands in Codex Alexandrinus, in Swete, and in Rahlfs; a reader who wants the Maccabean books together should find them together.\f + \ft Apostoliki Diakonia, “Μακκαβαίων Δ’ Παράρτημα,” note following chapter 1, verse 35; Swete, \+it Old Testament in Greek\+it*, vol. 3, title page.\f* With Daniel counted as one book with its additions, and the Epistle of Jeremias and the Prayer of Manasses counted separately, the Old Testament has 51 books and the Bible 78. The New Testament keeps the order of the English Bible, with Hebrews after Philemon and the Catholic Epistles after it, rather than the early Greek order that Robinson and Pierpont print; the English order is the order of the translation our New Testament rests on, and of the Orthodox New Testaments published in English.
\p We name the books as the Orthodox Church names them, in the forms the King James tradition had already given those names. The histories are the four books of Kingdoms, not Samuel and Kings, with the familiar names added in the full titles to help readers identify them; Joshua is Jesus the Son of Navi, the Greek Ἰησοῦς Ναυῆ, as the Apostoliki Diakonia heads him and as the King James New Testament itself calls him at {ACT 7:45} and {HEB 4:8}; the prophets are Esaias, Jeremias, Michaias, Abdias, Abbacum, Sophonias, Aggeus, Zacharias, and Malachias. Brenton used most of these forms himself. Where the present edition departs from him it is toward a form the King James tradition already had for the same name in its Greek books, or nearer the Greek itself: Ezekiel is spelled as {SIR 49:8} spells him in the King James Apocrypha, not Brenton’s Jezekiel; Nahum and Abdias in forms the King James Apocrypha also has, in the King James Bible’s 2 Esdras 1:39–40, for Brenton’s Naum and Obdias; Aggeus as {1ES 6:1} spells him, and Michaias as the Greek Μιχαίας is spelled, where Brenton wrote the name with a Latin ligature; Abbacum, for Brenton’s Ambacum, as the English service books spell him; and Hosea, in place of Brenton’s Osee, for recognizability, as explained below. The table that follows sets the names of the present edition beside the King James Bible’s wherever they differ or either Bible lacks the book; a book the table does not name has the same name in both.
{names}
\p The spelling of personal and place names serves two purposes: to preserve the Greek forms familiar from the New Testament and the services, and to give each bearer a consistent, recognizable name in both Testaments. The King James tradition already supplies the conventions. The translators spelled a name as the language they were translating spelled it: Isaiah, Elijah, and Hosea in the Old Testament, which they translated from the Hebrew; Esaias, Elias, and Osee in the New Testament, which they translated from the Greek. Since both Testaments here are Greek, the Greek forms are the starting point throughout the present edition. The prophet who is Esaias in Matthew is Esaias in his own book; Elias, Jeremias, Jonas, Core, and Melchisedek keep their Greek forms throughout, Melchisedek with the kappa of Μελχισεδέκ where both translators wrote the Latin Melchisedec; and the names are then the names the English services use, since the services were translated from the same Greek. We keep Brenton’s forms wherever the King James tradition supplies no established English form.
\p The King James translators themselves anglicized many Greek forms, Abraham for Ἀβραάμ, Noah for Νῶε at {HEB 11:7}, Judah for Ἰούδα at {SIR 49:4}, Bethlehem, Pharaoh, Sodom, Sinai, Tyre, and Sidon, and Brenton, transliterating afresh, often did not: he writes Abraam, Noe, Juda, Bethleem, Pharao, Sodoma, Sina. Where the King James New Testament or Apocrypha has an anglicized form of a Greek name, we use that form for its bearer in both Testaments, so that Abraham is Abraham in Genesis and in Matthew. Where the two Testaments of the King James Bible themselves differ for the same person, we prefer the form its Greek books use. Fifteen names depart from that rule, each for a reason its decision states. Hosea replaces Osee for recognizability, and for the same reason Gideon, Boaz, Ahaz, Sarah, and Issachar stand in the forms of the King James Old Testament, by which English readers know them, in place of the Greek books’ Gedeon, Booz, Achaz, Sara, and Isachar. Hezekias for Ἑζεκίας and Elisseus for Ἐλισσαῖος stand nearer the Greek, with its rough breathing and its double consonant, than the King James Ezekias and Eliseus, as Symeon for Συμεών and Melchisedek for Μελχισεδέκ stand nearer it than Simeon and Melchisedec. Phoenicia is the English form of Φοινίκη, in place of the King James Phenice, which English no longer uses for the region; Thaeman, the Greek Θαιμάν, takes one spelling where Brenton also wrote Thaman and Theman, and the King James Teman renders the Hebrew; Phineas is the English form of Φινεές, where the King James Old Testament has the Hebrew Phinehas; Mathusalah writes Luke’s Μαθουσάλα with the final letter the King James Old Testament gives the Hebrew name; and Navi is the services’ form, as the heading of the book explained above.
\p We respell a name by the person or place it names, not by its letters: the name changes in every verse where that bearer appears and in no other. There are about 100 such names. Where the same letters belong to another bearer, two rules decide. A respelling that changes only a name’s form reaches every bearer to whom the Greek and the Hebrew both give that name, since the split exists in neither Scripture: the Levite Hebron is spelled as the city is, the false prophet Sedecias as the king, and Reuben’s son Esrom as Phares’s son, since both are Hezron in the Hebrew and the Greek of the Pentateuch writes them alike. But a bearer whose Hebrew name differs, whom the Greek translator alone wrote like another, keeps Brenton’s form, since the identification is the translator’s and the English reader knows him by another name: the town of Zabulon that the Hebrew calls Kitron stays Kedron at {JDG 1:30} while the brook is Cedron, as {JHN 18:1} spells it; and Josias’s father is Amon throughout, as {MAT 1:10} spells him, though Brenton writes him Amos in Kingdoms and Jeremias, while the prophet Amos and the ancestor of Christ in Luke 3 keep their own name. Two names are confined by decision: Jesus is the son of Navi alone, and we leave the other men Brenton calls Jesus or Joshua unchanged; and Hannah is Samuel’s mother only. And where the Greek itself gives one bearer two names, we keep both: the king of Judah is Azarias in 4 Kingdoms and in {1CH 3:12}, where the Greek has Ἀζαρίας, and Ozias in 2 Chronicles and the prophets, where it has Ὀζίας, because those are two names and not, like Ἀμών and Ἀμώς, two spellings of one; and the mountain is Tabor where the Greek has Θαβώρ and Itabyrion at {HOS 5:1} and {JER 26:18}, where it has Ἰταβύριον. And where the Greek gives two bearers two names, we keep the difference, whatever the Hebrew behind it: Phineas, Φινεές, but Phinees for the temple servants’ house at {1ES 5:31}, Φινόε; Phoenicia, Φοινίκη, but Phenice for the haven of Crete at {ACT 27:12}, Φοῖνιξ; Salmanasar in 4 Kingdoms, Σαλαμανασσάρ, but Enemessar in Tobit, Ἐνεμεσσάρ. Declension is not a second name. The Greek never declines the name of Joseph’s son and his tribe, Μανασσή, and declines the king of Judah’s in half his verses, Μανασσῆς, and Brenton made that difference of grammar a difference of name, Manasse for the tribe and Manasses for the king; but both are Manasseh in the Hebrew, the Greek gives the king the tribe’s form as often as not, and the New Testament declines the tribe’s name at {REV 7:6} as it declines the king’s at {MAT 1:10}, so all are Manasses, the King James Bible’s form in both places, as Josias and Sedecias are each one name declined or undeclined. Two Greek spellings of one bearer are likewise one name, as Ἀμών and Ἀμώς are: Ῥαχάβ at {MAT 1:5} and Ῥαάβ at {HEB 11:31} are the same woman, and she is Rahab in Matthew as in Hebrews and in Jesus the Son of Navi. We leave the names in the readings and remarks of Brenton’s own notes as he wrote them, since a note is often about the very name it gives. In the prefaces and Brenton’s Introduction, we use Esaias, Aggeus, Esdras, and Zorobabel. Their other names stand as their authors wrote them, except that Brenton’s Preface follows his Introduction in writing Aristeas.
\s1 Chapters and Verses
\p The chapters and verses are the Septuagint’s, because a verse should be found under the number that the Church of Greece, a Greek Orthodox Bible, or a Septuagint edition gives it, and never under the Masoretic Hebrew’s number unless the Greek editions use it too. We number the Psalms as the Greek does, with Psalm 151, and a psalm’s title counts as its first verse or two, as Swete and Rahlfs count it; the chapters of Jeremias from 25 onward stand in the Greek order; we letter the additions to Esther within the chapters they follow; and we divide the Twelve Prophets and the other books as the Greek does, so that Joel has four chapters and Malachias four, as the Church of Greece prints them, with Malachias 4 ending, in the Greek order, with the sending of Elias before the charge concerning Moses. For a reader who knows the King James numbers, the website publishes tables of chapters and verses giving the King James Bible’s number wherever it differs, with the verses that one Bible has and the other lacks.
\p No single source gives the Septuagint’s numbering. We cannot follow Brenton’s own pages, because many of his numbers are borrowed: in the Apocrypha he printed the King James Apocrypha, with its division; in 3 Kingdoms 4–11 he printed the King James numbers out of their order; in Jeremias 25–51 he printed the Hebrew’s numbers; in the Psalms he did not count the titles and restarted Psalms 9 and 113 at the Hebrew’s break; and in Proverbs 24–31 he printed the King James numbers in the Greek order; elsewhere his pages leave a run of verses unnumbered or number one twice. We cannot follow Rahlfs alone, though his is the modern standard, because at several chapter breaks, Genesis 32, Exodus 22, and Jeremias 9 among them, he follows the Hebrew against Brenton, Swete, and the Church of Greece alike; an Orthodox edition should not be Masoretic where the Greek tradition is not. We cannot follow Swete alone, because he numbers Codex Vaticanus, and wherever Vaticanus lacks or joins words that Brenton’s English has, his numbers would divide the English wrongly. And we cannot follow the Church of Greece’s text alone in the form we consulted on the Apostoliki Diakonia website, because the website has many slips, a 9 for 19, a dropped numeral, a chapter’s first verse printed as the last of the chapter before.
\p We therefore adopt a rule of agreement, with the Church of Greece as the Orthodox standard. We start from Rahlfs’s numbering, in which Brenton’s text now circulates, and print the Church of Greece’s number wherever a second witness, Swete or Brenton’s own printed page, confirms it; that restores the traditional chapter breaks and the numbers Rahlfs letters or omits. We divide a verse otherwise only where the Church of Greece and Swete agree on the new division and Rahlfs or Brenton agrees with them, and only where Brenton’s English can take the division, that is, where his sentence or clause begins. Where only the Church of Greece gives a number, we keep Rahlfs’s number. A verse supplied from Codex Alexandrinus keeps the number Swete or Brenton’s Appendix gives it. There are about 100 relabellings and as many redivisions, each documented with its witnesses, and the tables on the present edition’s website show the result.
\p We accept the few anomalies the rule produces. Where the witnesses number the following verses but we keep Brenton’s division at the place itself, we leave a number unused rather than give it to words no witness gives it to: among them, the Epistle of Jeremias runs from 15 to 17, because the rejected verse 16 would begin inside Brenton’s clause, and Exodus 25 from 34 to 36, because Brenton prints within verse 34 the clause that the Church of Greece and Swete number 35. {EXO 40:8} keeps the anointing and the hallowing of the altar together, leaving 9 unused, because neither Brenton nor Rahlfs divides them, though the Church of Greece and Swete do. The Song of the Three Children runs on as Daniel 3:24–90, as Swete and Rahlfs number it and every Septuagint edition cites it, not as a separate 1–67. Where Brenton’s division is his own and the Greek editions place a verse’s edge only a phrase or a clause away from his, his division stands, since the verse is still found under its number.
\p A few places need further explanation. Proverbs in the Greek sets three passages that the Hebrew has at the end of the book, the Hebrew’s 30:1–14, 30:15–33, and 31:1–9, within chapter 24, so that the Greek chapter 31 begins at verse 10. We keep the Greek order and print those passages where the Greek has them, but under the Hebrew’s numbers, as the Church of Greece, Rahlfs, and Brenton all print them: the first verse of each passage shows its chapter, 30:1, 30:15, and 31:1, and the first verse back in chapter 24 shows 24:23. After {bare PRO 29:27} the text passes to 31:10, and a note there tells the reader that the Hebrew has the three passages at that point and that we print them in chapter 24. The reader who comes with a King James reference finds the verse; the reader who comes with a Septuagint edition finds the order. Daniel has Susanna before chapter 1 and Bel and the Dragon after chapter 12, each under its own heading without a chapter numeral, with numbered verses; the Prayer of Azarias and the Song stand within chapter 3. Sirach’s Prologue is the book’s introduction, unnumbered, as the Church of Greece prints it, so that the book’s first verse is {bare SIR 1:1}; and the Epistle of Jeremias has an unnumbered superscription before verse 1, as the Church of Greece and Swete print it. In the New Testament the only differences from the King James numbering are those the Byzantine text makes: the four verse numbers passed over, the doxology at {ROM 14:24-26}, and the exchanged verses of Matthew 23.
\s1 Paragraphs and Poetry
\p We set the text in paragraphs, the verse numbers marking the sense without breaking it, because this is a Bible for reading. Scrivener, who set the King James Version so in 1873, called paragraphing “in substance only a return to the fashion that prevailed in our early versions, before the Genevan New Testament of 1557 unfortunately broke up the text into divisions at once so minute and so arbitrary as the verses invented by Robert Stephens,” and quoted with approval an earlier paragraph Bible on the “very erroneous impression, that the Bible is rather a collection of apophthegms, or disconnected sentences, than composed of regular histories and treatises on religion.”\f + \ft Scrivener, \+it Cambridge Paragraph Bible\+it*, lix–lx.\f* The reference Bible of the last two centuries, two columns of numbered verses with a column of cross-references between them, was built, as Mark Bertrand observes, for looking things up, and “the easier they are to look things up in, the harder they are to read.” The present edition is a reader’s edition: a single column of paragraphed text with the least apparatus that will serve.\f + \ft Bertrand, “Reformation or Revolution”; Bertrand, “Look of the Bible.”\f* Both translations were printed in paragraphs, so the Old Testament keeps Brenton’s and the New Testament Scrivener’s. Each chapter opens a new paragraph, so that a chapter has a visible beginning even where the source ran on; within a chapter a continuation, a repeated formula, or an immediate reply may stand flush left while we indent a new movement of narrative or discourse.
\p Poetry was the one thing the two translations did not share. Scrivener set the poetry of the King James Bible in parallel lines “according to the principles first enunciated by Bishop Lowth,” with stanza divisions by sense and the Hebrew letters at the acrostics, and the New Testament here keeps his lines, in the Beatitudes, the canticles of Luke 1 and 2, the tribes sealed in Revelation 7, and, in Revelation 18, the fall of Babylon.\f + \ft Scrivener, \+it Cambridge Paragraph Bible\+it*, lx.\f* Brenton printed the whole Septuagint as prose, Psalms and Job and Esaias alike. Boyd’s 2020 \it Updated Brenton English Septuagint\it* supplies what Brenton lacked: the last of the five light revisions he made to Brenton was that “poetic formatting has been introduced for poetic portions of Scripture,” and because Boyd’s text closely follows Brenton’s, we can align his line and stanza divisions with Brenton’s words, settling uncertain breaks by documented decisions.\f + \ft Boyd, introduction to \+it Updated Brenton\+it*, “Revisions to the Text.”\f* We take only the places where his lines begin and end, and print no word of his revision.
\p We choose which passages to set as poetry where Boyd and Scrivener agree. Outside the Apocrypha, we set a verse of Brenton’s in lines where Boyd sets it in lines and Scrivener also sets its King James counterpart in lines, or where the verse has no counterpart in the King James Bible; where Scrivener sets the counterpart as prose, prose stands. In the Apocrypha, whose verses the King James Bible numbers otherwise and whose Tobit and Sirach follow another text, Boyd’s lines stand on their own. Two boundaries we settle by decision against the rule. At {ECC 3:9} both set the question about labour as a line of verse, and we keep it prose, where Brenton’s own printing begins a new paragraph after the poem of the seasons; at {SIR 50:27} Boyd sets Sirach’s colophon as verse, and we keep it prose, as Scrivener prints it, though Scrivener has no vote in the Apocrypha. Two special forms follow from the lines: the terminal “Pause.” of the Psalms and of Habakkuk 3 stands on its own line at the right, and the acrostic names of Lamentations stand as centred headings. Where a small-capital title opens one of Brenton’s prose paragraphs, we set it apart in italic as a descriptive title.
\p We divide the Psalter into the 20 kathismata and 60 stases in which the Orthodox Church reads it, under the Septuagint’s numbering, with Psalm 151 standing outside the divisions. Archimandrite Ephrem Lash, reproaching an Orthodox study Bible that had printed the Hebrew Psalter, said that its editors “could at least have printed the Psalms with the correct numbering and divided them into the traditional kathismata and staseis of the Church Psalter,” which “does not even require a knowledge of Greek, only access to Miss Hapgood’s compendium of Orthodox services.”\f + \ft Lash, “Orthodox Study Bible,” paragraph beginning “When we turn to the text of the Psalter.”\f* We set a kathisma heading in small capitals, mark a stasis with a rule, and in the 17th kathisma, which is Psalm 118 alone, we give headings to the Second and Third Stases and mark the “Middle” at verse 94, as the Psalter is read at the Midnight Office and at funerals. The divisions are a frame for reading, not a change to the psalms: Psalm 71 keeps its closing notice, “The hymns of David the son of Jesse are ended,” as its last verse, and we add nothing to the text.
\s1 The Notes
\p We preserve both translations’ notes, adapting them to the present text and to the conventions described below. Brenton printed footnotes giving the literal Greek, the Hebrew where it differs, the readings of Codex Alexandrinus, and the renderings he had considered, and an Appendix of longer notes and passages; the King James translators printed in their 1611 margin the literal Greek and the alternative renderings they had weighed, which Scrivener counted at 765 for the New Testament.\f + \ft Scrivener, \+it Cambridge Paragraph Bible\+it*, xxx.\f* We include some 5,000 notes from both sources and set the eight long notes of Brenton’s Appendix as footnotes at the verses they discuss, so that the Appendix is no longer a detached section at the back. To them we add about 1,250 notes of our own, every one the consequence of a change described in this introduction: the reading of Vaticanus or of Brenton displaced by Codex Alexandrinus, the reading of the Received Text displaced by the Byzantine text, the former King James words where we corrected the English, and the notes that say where a passage stands in the other text.
\p The notes stand in the inside margin beside their verse, without callers save the few set below the text, each beginning with the verse number and the words of the verse it is about, followed by a colon. A reader looks from a verse to the margin, not from a mark in the text to the foot of the page, and we leave the column of Scripture unbroken. The witness labels are few, and the abbreviated ones are in the list of abbreviations: \it Gr.\it* introduces the literal Greek; \it Heb.\it* the Hebrew; \it Alex.\it* and \it Vat.\it* the two codices; \it Brenton\it* the English of Brenton’s printed text where a manuscript cannot be named; \it TR\it* the Received Text and \it KJV\it* the King James Version, as the preceding section distinguishes them; \it or,\it* another rendering of the same words; \it lit.\it* the literal rendering; \it i.e.\it* that is, \it q.d.\it* as if to say, and \it sc.\it* that is to say, as Brenton used them; and \it See\it* and \it Compare\it* before a cross-reference. We set alternative renderings, cited words, and glosses in italic without quotation marks, and labels and comments in roman; a note runs on from its colon in lowercase; a complete sentence ends with a stop, and a fragment with none. When we offer another rendering in a note, we quote enough of the verse for the reader to substitute the alternative without ambiguity or a break in the sentence; the same convention applies in “again: or, \it from above\it*” at {JHN 3:7} as in “sons of God: Alex. \it angels of God\it*” at {GEN 6:2}. Brenton printed his notes with labels and cited words alike in italic, while the 1611 margin had its own conventions; we bring both to this one style, using the present edition’s conventions for citations and book names, so that readers need learn only one apparatus.
\p We keep a 1611 note wherever its words are still in the text. Where we now print the Byzantine reading the margin offered, as at {MAT 26:26}, whose margin had “Many Greek copies have, gave thanks,” we omit the margin note, and the Received Text note records the former reading in its place; where the margin glossed words the text no longer has, it goes with them, or, where the words changed but the note still applies, we set it on the new words; and where a correction of the English adopts the margin’s own alternative, we reverse the note, giving the former text after “or,” as the margin had given the alternative. The translators’ textual remark at {LUK 17:35}, “This 36th verse is wanting in most of the Greek copies,” stands beside the note that quotes the verse. Where we adopt a reading of Alexandrinus from Brenton’s note, we rewrite the note to give the displaced reading, as the section on Codex Alexandrinus explains; one that gives a reading we do not print stands as he wrote it; and we drop those that only sent the reader to the Appendix, since the Appendix has come to the verse.
\s1 The Quotation Links
\p Where the New Testament quotes the Old, we link the two passages at both ends: the first verse of the quotation names the passage quoted, and the first verse of the passage names the quotation, and, where Turpie’s class decides it, a sign says which text the quotation follows. The links put Grinfield’s argument into the margin. Grinfield showed that the quotations are the Septuagint’s; David McCalman Turpie, in his 1868 \it The Old Testament in the New\it*, tabulated every quotation and classified it by its agreement with the Hebrew and the Septuagint, using Brenton’s version for the Greek, “corrected or improved when required,” and Tischendorf’s text for the New Testament.\f + \ft Turpie, \+it Old Testament in the New\+it*, viii, xx.\f* His five classes are these: A, quotations that agree with the Hebrew where the Septuagint renders it correctly; B, that agree with the Hebrew where the Septuagint does not; C, that differ from the Hebrew where the Septuagint renders it correctly; D, that differ from the Hebrew but agree with the Septuagint where it departs from the Hebrew; and E, that differ from both where the two differ from each other.\f + \ft Turpie, \+it Old Testament in the New\+it*, xvi.\f* Class D is the one that matters to Grinfield’s argument: the places where the Apostles follow the Greek against the Hebrew, as the Epistle to the Hebrews does when it quotes “a body hast thou prepared me” from Psalm 39. The links print Turpie’s judgment as a gloss after the reference, in the three glosses, made of two signs, that the following table explains, or leave the reference alone where his class gives no verdict between the two texts, so that the reader sees at {HOS 11:1} that Matthew follows the Hebrew where the Septuagint differs, and at {PSA 39:7} that Hebrews follows the Septuagint where the Hebrew differs.
{glosses}
\p We link 272 of the 282 rows of Turpie’s tables. We leave out ten: five for which he names no settled Old Testament passage, three that he himself says are not quotations, one that he repeats, and {MAT 27:35}, whose quotation of {PSA 21:19} the Byzantine text lacks, so that the verse no longer quotes the psalm and its words stand only in the Received Text note. Where the Byzantine text shortens a quotation, as at {ROM 13:9}, the words that remain still quote the passage and the link stands. We drop Brenton’s own cross-references to the New Testament, a few dozen “See” notes, where a link now names the same verses, and keep them where they say more. Turpie compared Tischendorf’s Greek New Testament with the Hebrew and with Brenton’s Vaticanus Septuagint; his classes judge those texts, not the present edition’s English or its readings from Alexandrinus and the Byzantine text, and a quotation marked as agreeing may still differ in English wording.
\s1 Spelling, Grammar, and Punctuation
\p We adjust the two translations, made more than two centuries apart, with two aims: that the English of both Testaments be the English of the Orthodox services, and that it be one English. We leave everything else alone. We state the rules here in the order of their importance; each applies across the whole of Scripture.
\p The vocabulary of the services comes first. \it Holy Ghost\it* becomes \it Holy Spirit\it* throughout, and \it ghost\it* becomes \it spirit\it* wherever the word occurs, following the usage of Orthodox service books; \it Hallelujah\it* becomes \it Alleluia\it*, the Greek form the liturgy uses. We keep the words Holy Transfiguration Monastery sets aside on theological grounds, \it propitiation\it* and \it atonement\it*, because the rule here is the services’ common usage, not a translator’s judgment of doctrine; the translators’ own notes stand beside them.
\p The grammar of the King James Bible comes next, and here we bring the two translations into one. The King James Version is consistent in its old inflections: \it hath\it*, \it saith\it*, \it doth\it*, the third person in \it -eth\it*, \it thou\it* with \it -est\it*. Brenton, writing in the same idiom, slipped: he has \it saith\it* on one page and \it says\it* on the next, \it hath\it* and \it has\it*, \it doth\it* and \it does\it*. We carry the King James inflections through both Testaments and the lemmas and readings quoted in the notes: \it says\it* becomes \it saith\it* and \it has\it* becomes \it hath\it* wherever Brenton wrote them in the text of Scripture, by nearly 800 rules of the form “says: saith.” Explanatory prose, Brenton’s preface and introduction, the translators’ preface, and this introduction keep their authors’ grammar, since they are not Scripture. We retain the archaic forms both translators used deliberately: \it spake\it* and \it begat\it* in the New Testament, \it builded\it*, \it digged\it*, \it holpen\it*, \it sware\it*, \it fishes\it*. Boyd modernizes most of these, \it begat\it* to \it begot\it* and \it spake\it* to \it spoke\it*, \it builded\it* to \it built\it*, \it digged\it* to \it dug\it*, on the ground that they “sound quite strange, if not incorrect, to modern ears”; they do not sound strange in church, where the services are full of them, and an edition made for the Church keeps the register the services keep.\f + \ft Boyd, introduction to \+it Updated Brenton\+it*, “Revisions to the Text”; Boyd, introduction to \+it American Standard Version Byzantine Text\+it*, paragraph beginning “A number of minor changes.”\f* The past tenses follow the inflections in five verbs. Brenton’s \it spoke\it*, \it begot\it*, \it bore\it*, \it broke\it*, and \it swore\it* become \it spake\it*, \it begat\it*, \it bare\it*, \it brake\it*, and \it sware\it* in the text of Scripture and in the readings the notes quote, as his \it says\it* gives way to \it saith\it*: the King James Bible holds to each of these forms without exception in the books of its canon and uses them constantly, \it spake\it* more than six hundred times and \it spoke\it* never; its Apocrypha, which Brenton reprinted, has \it swore\it* twice and \it broke\it* once, and the prevailing form is carried there too. The participles stand, \it spoken\it* and \it begotten\it*, and so does \it bore\it* where it means to pierce. The King James Bible is as constant in \it tare\it*, \it forgat\it*, \it trode\it*, and \it ware\it*, but has them in a handful of verses, four, nine, eight, and one, and the services hardly at all; these four we modernize wherever either Bible has them, in the notes and prefaces as in the text, \it tare\it* to \it tore\it* in Mark and Luke, \it trode\it* to \it trod\it*, \it forgat\it* to \it forgot\it* in Wisdom, and \it ware\it* to \it wore\it* in Luke and Judith, as the King James \it strake sail\it* is \it struck sail\it*. The choice is of which forms to carry, and the line is drawn where the counts leave a gap, between \it brake\it* at seventy-seven and \it forgat\it* at nine. Where the translators themselves used both forms, \it drave\it* and \it drove\it*, \it gat\it* and \it got\it*, \it sang\it* and \it sung\it*, each translator’s own stands.
\p We choose possessive forms and articles by sound. \it My\it* and \it thy\it* stand before a consonant sound, \it mine\it* and \it thine\it* before a vowel sound, exactly as \it a\it* and \it an\it* do: \it mine eyes\it*, \it thine arm\it*, \it mine own country\it*, but \it my heart\it*, \it thy house\it*; a silent \it h\it* takes \it mine\it*, \it mine honour\it*, \it mine hour of need\it*, and an intervening adjective governs the form, \it mine old age\it*. \it A\it* and \it an\it* follow the same rule: \it a house\it*, \it a hundred\it*, but \it an hour\it*, \it an heir\it*, \it an honour\it*. Scrivener saw the principle, that “my and mine, thy and thine, should of course be used respectively as a and an before a consonant, or vowel, or h,” but kept the inconsistent practice of 1611 because no editor had ever applied the rule; he applied it to the article except for \it an hundred\it*.\f + \ft Scrivener, \+it Cambridge Paragraph Bible\+it*, li–lii.\f* We apply it to both, in both Testaments, by the sound of the following word as it is pronounced today, so that the text reads aloud as the reader naturally reads it. We leave the independent possessives unchanged: “the silver is mine,” “thine is the kingdom.”
\p We revise obsolete spellings for ease of reading; Holy Transfiguration Monastery says the same of the King James Version’s obsolete words, and Scrivener judged that the editors who had gradually cleared the 1611 page of its “uncouth, obsolete, and variable forms, which could answer no purpose save to perplex the ignorant, and offend the educated taste,” were “right in the main.”\f + \ft Scrivener, \+it Cambridge Paragraph Bible\+it*, xlvi.\f* \it Shew\it* is \it show\it* and \it shewed\it* is \it showed\it* or \it shown\it*; \it alway\it* is \it always\it*, \it amongst\it* is \it among\it*, \it throughly\it* is \it thoroughly\it*, \it subtil\it* is \it subtle\it*, \it brasen\it* is \it brazen\it*, \it spue\it* is \it spew\it*; the eighteenth-century \it -ick\it* of \it apostolick\it* and \it musick\it* becomes \it -ic\it*, \it emerods\it* becomes \it haemorrhoids\it*, and we regularize many other forms in the same way, each by a rule that names the word. We write the Latin ligatures as their two letters wherever the sources print them, in names and in Latin alike, as the \it ae\it* of \it haemorrhoids\it* and of Thaeman. Two rules decide a spelling that English still writes. Where both translations write it and neither writes it otherwise, it stands: the British \it honour\it*, \it labour\it*, and \it Saviour\it*, which the services have too, and \it fulness\it*, which the translators and Brenton spell alike in every one of its occurrences. Where the two translations spell a word differently, or either of them wavers, one form serves both, the King James Bible’s where it keeps to one, \it inquire\it* for Brenton’s \it enquire\it*, \it gray\it* for his \it grey\it*, and \it wagon\it* for his \it waggon\it*, and otherwise the form now usual, \it among\it* for \it amongst\it* and \it connection\it* for the \it connexion\it* Brenton wrote beside it. A form that English has given up is modernized as \it shew\it* is, whichever Bible has it and however constantly: \it expence\it*, \it scrole\it*, \it dulness\it*. A word that a translator did not translate but transliterated is not a spelling but a name, and follows the names: Brenton’s \it theraphin\it* keeps the Greek θεραφείν, where the King James \it teraphim\it* renders the Hebrew and its Greek books lack the word. We keep a spelling that carries a meaning, and distinguish by sense the pairs the sources confuse: \it besides\it* for addition and \it beside\it* for position, \it borne\it* for bearing and \it born\it* for birth, \it farther\it* for distance and \it further\it* for continuation, \it diverse\it* for different in kind and \it divers\it* for several, \it clothes\it* for garments and \it cloths\it* for pieces of cloth, \it stayed\it* for the verb and \it staid\it* for the adjective. We retain one old form for its meaning: Scrivener’s text has the translators’ \it shamefastness\it* at {1TI 2:9}, where later printers had put \it shamefacedness\it*, and we carry \it shamefast\it* into Brenton’s Apocrypha to preserve that meaning of modesty.
\p We hyphenate less than Brenton did, for the reason Boyd gives: “Nineteenth century works make much greater use of the hyphen than what is commonly accepted today, which can be a bit distracting for the modern reader.”\f + \ft Boyd, introduction to \+it Updated Brenton\+it*, “Revisions to the Text.”\f* We revise compound spellings by explicit decisions. English writes a compound three ways, and the two translations between them write the same compound in all three, \it sweetsmelling\it*, \it sweet-smelling\it*, and \it sweet smelling\it*; the common compounds revised here take the form English now gives them. A compound that English has closed is closed: \it today\it*, \it anything\it*, \it beforehand\it*, \it tonight\it*, \it birthday\it*, \it breastplate\it*, \it honeycomb\it*, \it storehouse\it*. A compound modifier is hyphenated before its noun and open after it, \it sweet-smelling\it* savour, \it whole-burnt\it* offering, \it free-will\it* offering, but \it well pleasing\it* to God; a compound that English hyphenates wherever it stands keeps its hyphen after the noun too, \it stiff-necked\it*, \it like-minded\it*, \it high-minded\it*, \it double-tongued\it*, \it long-suffering\it*, \it mother-in-law\it*. Other declared changes open a compound: \it fig tree\it*, \it high priest\it*, \it Sabbath day\it*, \it barley harvest\it*, \it fellow servant\it*, and every offering of the Law, \it burnt offering\it*, \it sin offering\it*, \it peace offering\it*, \it meat offering\it*, \it drink offering\it*, \it heave offering\it*, \it wave offering\it*, \it trespass offering\it*. The King James Bible, which writes \it fig tree\it* forty times and the offerings open without exception, shows these to be the ordinary forms and not ours; where it closes a compound that English has since opened or hyphenated, \it fellowservant\it*, \it stiffnecked\it*, \it longsuffering\it*, the present form stands there too. \it First-born\it*, \it first-fruits\it*, \it corner-stone\it*, \it mercy-seat\it*, \it stumbling-block\it*, \it stumbling-stone\it*, \it threshing-floor\it*, \it dwelling-place\it*, \it ewe-lamb\it*, \it turtle-dove\it*, \it pre-eminence\it*, \it battle-array\it*, and \it well-pleased\it* retain their hyphens, with their plurals, wherever they stand, by editorial preference. We also close the numbered compounds in \it -fold\it*, as \it twofold\it* and \it hundredfold\it*, and write \it lawgiver\it* and \it all-wise\it*, with \it round about\it* open. We hyphenate \it only-begotten\it* before a noun; \it the only begotten of the Father\it* stays open. Compounds outside the declared changes keep their sources’ forms, including differences between the translations. These rules change no word.
\p Brenton punctuated as his century did, and Boyd describes that system exactly: “Nineteenth century writers understood the comma, semicolon, colon, and period to represent pauses of increasing length,” and so used semicolons where a modern reader expects a comma, colons for an appended remark, and lowercase after a question mark or exclamation.\f + \ft Boyd, introduction to \+it Updated Brenton\+it*, “Regarding Punctuation and Grammar.”\f* We leave that system in place, because it is the translator’s own and because it serves the voice: Scrivener found the pointing of 1611 too scanty to guide the voice in public reading and the modern pointing “heavier and more elaborate than necessity requires,” and chose to lighten the latter rather than replace it; Brenton’s pointing needs neither.\f + \ft Scrivener, \+it Cambridge Paragraph Bible\+it*, xli.\f* We remove redundant punctuation: a comma, semicolon, or colon that stands immediately before a dash or a parenthetical aside goes, as Boyd removes the comma in both his revisions, and where a mark inside a closing parenthesis belongs to the enclosing sentence, we move it outside.\f + \ft Boyd, introduction to \+it Updated Brenton\+it*, “Revisions to the Text”; Boyd, introduction to \+it American Standard Version Byzantine Text\+it*, paragraph beginning “A number of minor changes.”\f* A number or letter in parentheses that marks a verse or an item of a list is no aside, and the mark before it stays. We never change periods, question marks, or exclamation marks by rule. Where a single verse needed its own punctuation, because a rhetorical question had lost its mark or a parenthesis its sentence, we document the change as a decision at that verse; there are about 60. We also remove the six pairs of quotation marks that Brenton set around names and speech, since neither translation otherwise uses them. The name that Codex Alexandrinus adds at {EZK 48:35}, ‘the Lord is there,’ keeps its marks, because the clause stands as the subject of the sentence. In the New Testament the question marks follow the Robinson–Pierpont text where the Received Text pointed otherwise.
\p We print in ordinary type the words the translators supplied to complete the English sense, which the King James Bible prints in italics and Brenton partly in italics and partly in square brackets. Our reasons are those Boyd gives in his \it Text-Critical English New Testament\it*: no translation carries the practice out consistently, so that the italics mislead by their silences; a text that did carry it out “would be littered with italicized words, creating a visual distraction”; and “in modern English the use of italics implies emphasis, and it is awkward when relatively minor words seem to be emphasized.”\f + \ft Boyd, \+it Text-Critical English New Testament\+it*, vii.\f* Scrivener, who chose the other course, admitted that under the largest head of italics “it may reasonably be doubted whether much advantage accrues from a change of type where the sense is not affected to an appreciable extent.”\f + \ft Scrivener, \+it Cambridge Paragraph Bible\+it*, xxxvi.\f* We drop Brenton’s square brackets on the same principle. They serve two purposes. In the books the Hebrew Bible has, his brackets mark Greek that the Hebrew lacks, as in {GEN 35:16} and the additions to Esther; in a Greek Old Testament that Greek is the text, and the brackets would mark the Church’s Scripture as doubtful by a Hebrew standard we do not apply. Where he explained in a note what the Hebrew lacks, the note stays on those words. In the books the Hebrew Bible does not have, his brackets mark English supplied without Greek behind it, as the translators’ italics do; those words are supplied words like the others, and we print them alike. We keep italic in the text only for titles, the superscriptions of the Psalms and the descriptive titles of the prophets.
\p We reduce capitals and small capitals to ordinary use. We set in ordinary case the emphatic capitals of the King James Bible, JESUS at {MAT 1:21}, {LUK 1:31}, and {LUK 2:21}, the inscription on the cross, MYSTERY, BABYLON THE GREAT, KING OF KINGS, AND LORD OF LORDS, along with Brenton’s THE BEING and I AM; Scrivener found the capitals of 1611 “much more free … than at present” and reduced them, and these are the last of them.\f + \ft Scrivener, \+it Cambridge Paragraph Bible\+it*, liv.\f* The small capitals both translations used for selective emphasis go; a decorative opening of a book keeps its small capitals, and we set headings in them. Abbreviations and sigla, \it TR\it*, \it KJV\it*, \it LXX\it*, \it MS\it*, \it AD\it*, keep their capitals. We capitalize \it King\it* before a named ruler, as in \it King David\it*, and \it Mount\it* where it begins a mountain’s name, as in \it Mount Sinai\it* and the \it Mount of Olives\it*; generic references keep lowercase, and so do both words after an article or a demonstrative, \it the king Abimelech\it*, \it the mount Sion\it*, where the word is a common noun standing before the name, as in \it the river Jordan\it*, \it mount\it* after an adjective too, and in a description such as \it the mount of God\it*. We capitalize \it Sabbath\it* and \it Sabbaths\it*, including Sabbaths of years, and \it Scripture\it* and \it Scriptures\it* for the Biblical writings collectively and a particular passage. None of these changes touches a word.
\p These rules have limits. We modernize no word for being old: \it unto\it*, \it wherefore\it*, \it let\it* meaning hinder at {2TH 2:7} and {ROM 1:13}, \it conversation\it* meaning conduct, \it prevent\it* meaning go before, all stand, with the notes to explain them where the translators or Brenton explained them. We alter no rendering under these rules; a change of wording is a textual decision or a correction of the English, documented as such with its note, never a spelling change. The prefaces of Brenton and of the translators take the declared spelling, compound, article, and vocabulary changes, with names kept as described above, but retain their authors’ grammar. The notes likewise retain their authors’ grammar in commentary; quoted readings and lemmas follow the grammatical rules applied to Scripture. And we correct one misprint: at {NUM 21:5} the Bagster printings of Brenton read “ought of Egypt” where his 1844 edition reads “out,” and we print “out.”
\s1 The Printed Page
\p The page is ISO B5, 176 by 250 millimeters, with a single column of text and the notes in the inside margin beside their verses; the few notes too long for the margin, and the eight long notes of Brenton’s Appendix, stand at the foot of the page with lettered callers, and the notes of the prefaces with numbered ones. We set the text in Valkyrie at 9 points on an 11-point line, with the Greek of the notes in GFS Heraklit and the Hebrew in Ezra SIL, and hold the lines to a grid so that they register front and back through the paper. Chapter numbers are large and lining; the other figures are oldstyle; verse numbers are superior, and we omit the figure 1 at the start of a chapter. Headings and running heads are in small capitals, and the outer running head gives the first reference on an even page and the last on an odd one. The design is for reading at length, and nothing interrupts the column but the headings of the books, the Psalter’s divisions, the titles of Daniel’s additions and Sirach’s Prologue, and the acrostic headings of Lamentations: there are no callers but those of the few notes set below the text, no cross-reference column, and no section headings.
\s1 Abbreviations
\p The list below gives the few abbreviations used in the notes. A citation gives the abbreviated book name, chapter, and verse, with an en dash in a range and a semicolon between books; \it Ps.\it* introduces one psalm and \it Pss.\it* several. Of Brenton’s own list we keep the abbreviations the notes still use, reword some of their meanings, and drop the signs and labels the notes no longer print; we add those the present edition uses, and arrange them all alphabetically. The signs of the quotation links are not abbreviations; the section on the links explains them.
{abbreviations}
\s1 Acknowledgments
\p We owe the present edition to the work of others. Its English is Sir Lancelot Brenton’s and the King James translators’; its New Testament text and its paragraphs are Scrivener’s; its Greek New Testament is Robinson and Pierpont’s, and its first instructions for conforming the King James Version to it are William Pierpont’s own. The Revised Version supplied wording for changes in both Testaments. Robert Adam Boyd’s \it Updated Brenton English Septuagint\it* gave the lines of its Old Testament poetry, and his \it American Standard Version Byzantine Text\it* and \it Text-Critical English New Testament\it* supplied wording for the New Testament where the King James tradition had none. We verified every reading of Codex Alexandrinus against Henry Barclay Swete’s edition of the Septuagint, and David McCalman Turpie’s classification underlies the quotation links. We worked out the tables of chapters and verses on the website with the help of STEP Bible’s versification data. We are grateful to all of them; the choices, and the errors, are ours.
\p We have adapted English renderings from Robert Adam Boyd’s \it The Text-Critical English New Testament\it* (© 2021 Robert Adam Boyd), licensed under Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International (https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/), source edition https://archive.org/details/tcent. We have brought the borrowed wording into the English of the King James Version; as the licence requires of an adaptation, the present edition does not bear the names The Text-Critical English New Testament or Byzantine Text Version.
